    "content": "Trade within the East African Community (EAC) increased by 37% in the second quarter of 2026 compared to the same period in 2025, according to the EAC's quarterly statistical report covering April to June. The total value of regional trade reached $52.3 billion, up from $38.2 billion in the previous year.\n\nThe rise was largely supported by a 41.3% growth in exports, which amounted to $26.3 billion. Minerals and precious metals were the main drivers of this export growth, though the report did not provide a detailed breakdown by country or commodity.\n\nThe EAC, established in 2000, is a regional intergovernmental organization comprising seven member states: Burundi, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Kenya, Rwanda, South Sudan, Tanzania, and Uganda. Somalia joined the bloc in 2024, becoming its eighth member. The increase in trade reflects ongoing efforts to deepen economic integration and reduce trade barriers within the region.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, which maintains strong business and remittance ties across East Africa, this growth signals expanding market opportunities and potentially lower transaction costs. Many diaspora entrepreneurs are active in cross-border trade, particularly in livestock, agriculture, and retail, and could benefit from improved regional trade facilitation.\n\nThe EAC's report highlights the region's resilient economic trajectory despite global uncertainties. However, it remains to be seen whether this momentum can be sustained, especially given the volatility in global commodity prices that heavily influence export earnings.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/20/ganacsiga-bariga-afrika-oo-kordhay-37-gaarayna-52-3-bilyan/",
